{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011fGJCX3Dr3b3Ey9ALmUx6M","type":"tool_result","content":"102:### Chapter 3 — The Ship's Company\n103-\n104-- **Subtitle**: *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"*\n105-- **Purpose**: domain — D1.1 Kubernetes Fundamentals / cluster architecture (chapter_type: `content`)\n106-- **Covers**: **D1.1** — deployment eras; what Kubernetes is and is not; control-plane components (kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, cloud-controller-manager); node components (kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime); addons; controllers; **the control loop**; desired vs current state; Kubernetes origin and history\n107-- **Prerequisites**: Ch 2 — containers, container runtime, CRI\n108-- **Target length**: Content-driven, no word target. **Depth band: standard-plus** — 6 points, but it opens the book's structural spine and the control-loop treatment must be strong enough to carry five later callbacks\n109-- **Retrieval targets**: **10%** **[B3]** — the schedule's opening rung, drawn entirely from Ch 2. Anchors: the CRI boundary (which component actually talks to the runtime), image immutability, container-vs-VM\n110-- **Key concepts introduced**: **The control loop** (desired state, current state, reconciliation) — the single most reused idea in the book; the control-plane/node split; why no component is \"in charge\"; etcd as the state of record\n111-- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch03-fig01-control-plane-and-node-components`, `ch03-fig02-control-loop-desired-vs-current`, `ch03-fig03-deployment-eras-timeline`, `ch03-fig04-request-path-through-apiserver`, `ch03-zenith-nobody-is-in-charge`\n112-- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 19 Practice · **37 total**\n113-- **Notes**: `ch03-fig02` is the book's most reused figure — Ch 6, Ch 15, and Ch 17 all call back to its shape. Design it once, deliberately, for re-presentation at three later altitudes.\n114-\n115----\n116-\n117-### Chapter 4 — Records of Intent\n118-\n119-- **Subtitle**: *\"You don't give Kubernetes orders. You file a declaration.\"*\n120-- **Purpose**: domain — D1.1 Kubernetes Fundamentals / objects and configuration (chapter_type: `content`)\n121-- **Covers**: **D1.1** — objects; spec/status; manifests; `apiVersion`/`kind`/`metadata`/`spec`; `kubectl apply`; labels; selectors (equality and set-based); annotations; namespaces; initial namespaces; **namespaced vs cluster-scoped**; ConfigMaps; Secrets (definition and contrast)\n122-- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 — control loop, kube-apiserver, etcd as state of record\n123-- **Target length**: Content-driven, no word target. **Depth band: standard-plus** — 6 points, but two of the book's nine cross-cutting themes originate here (namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped; labels/selectors as the universal join)\n124-- **Retrieval targets**: **15%** **[B3]** — from Ch 2–3. Anchors: the control loop (what does the controller compare `spec` against?), image references inside a Pod spec, the apiserver's role in `apply`\n125-- **Key concepts introduced**: Declarative desired state as an artifact, not a command; `spec` vs `status` as the control loop's two halves; labels and selectors as the mechanism that joins nearly every object pair in Kubernetes; the namespaced/cluster-scoped boundary; ConfigMap vs Secret (and that Secrets are only base64-encoded)\n126-- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch04-fig01-object-anatomy-spec-status`, `ch04-fig02-apply-round-trip`, `ch04-fig03-labels-selectors-join`, `ch04-fig04-namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped`, `ch04-fig05-configmap-secret-contrast`, `ch04-zenith-declaration-not-order`\n127-- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 19 Practice · **37 total**\n128-- **Notes**: Cached coverage is sufficient — no blocking gaps. `ch04-fig04` is load-bearing for Ch 12: **[B3]** the RBAC four-way matrix (Role/ClusterRole × RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding) should be *derived* from this 